//! check: run(exit=1, stdout="handled: fallback\nremoved-absent: handled\nerror: not_found")
//! phase: run
//! conforms: std.fs, err.row, err.propagate, err.else
//!
//! The `not_found` litmus, in both directions a caller can take it: an
//! `else` handler that turns the miss into a value and lets the program
//! continue, and a `?` that propagates it out of `main` — where the
//! process outcome NAMES the tag on stdout and exits 1. That last line is
//! how this rig witnesses a tag's identity: an `else` handler cannot say
//! WHICH tag fired (a bare-identifier handler binds every one of them and
//! a payload pattern is a wolfc rejection, F-0043), so the propagation
//! record is the only place the tag appears.
//!
//! Deterministic on any host: the path names a directory that does not
//! exist inside a scratch directory staging has just cleared. Removing an
//! absent file is a miss for the same reason — `remove` does not pretend
//! that "there was nothing to do" is success.
//!
//! `denied` still has no portable litmus: making a file unreadable needs
//! a permission call the language does not have. Its meaning is documented
//! on the functions and nothing here claims to have observed it. **`utf8`
//! is no longer in that sentence** — making a file un-decodable needs
//! byte-level writing, which is what s90 added, and `utf8_row.lu`
//! witnesses the tag the same way this file witnesses `not_found`.

use std.fs

fn main() -> !int {
    let absent = "no-such-dir/absent.txt"
    let text = fs.read_text(absent) else |_| "fallback"
    print("handled: {text}")
    let gone = "never-existed.tmp"
    var removed = "not-handled"
    fs.remove(gone) else |_| {
        removed = "handled"
    }
    print("removed-absent: {removed}")
    let strict = fs.read_text(absent)?
    print("unreachable: {strict}")
    0
}
